# Let the Darkness Depart! - Part 1
Tamil title: இருளகல! - பகுதி 1

From the vast darkness, tiny fragments of light burst forth. The enveloping darkness began to lose its form like a vessel pierced with holes. The released fragments wandered here and there, attracting one another. One form became another, while many forms embraced and became a vast form; a great transformation continued to unfold. Seeing the fragments that had broken it apart now roaming in ever-new forms, the shattered darkness stood agitated, unable to change its condition or regain its former unbroken immensity.

Perhaps the vast darkness thought: what has gone will not return; authority will not come back; it is enough if what remains can endure, if only further disintegration can be prevented.

The darkness that had ruled for ages had never imagined that forces existed capable of driving it away, scattering it, and destroying its form. So profound was its faith in its own dominion.

Where did the fragments of light arise? Where had they been imprisoned until then, and why? What power had restrained them? What weakened that power? How did the fragments acquire new strength?

Immense noise: the collective outcry of many kinds of sound. Roaring, growling, hissing, bellowing, barking, and wailing joined together and produced a mighty clamour. Yet within it, one kind of sound at a time became stronger than the others. Only the keenest attention could detect it.

Sounds emerged from the clamour and acquired order. Each assumed its own pattern and, leaving the meaningless state called noise, gained meaning.

Noise found refuge and survival among beasts and birds. Humans, however, worked upon it as stone is carved into statues and tools. They made it language, sweetened it into music, and, according to emotion and intended action, disciplined it into meaningful sound for war and uprising. In the forest there remained noise; in the country there arose music, speech, conversation, argument, shouting, lament, and weeping. As though warning us not to imagine that only noise belongs to the forest or that music belongs to us alone, the skylark circles and sings.

Thus ordered and meaningful languages, signs, and music were born from immense noise. They had lain confined within that noise, yet found release, classification, and meaning. How?

Peace is born from great confusion. Confusion later becomes systems and receives order. While it remains confusion it has neither purpose nor meaning; once systematised, it acquires both.

From the many explanations offered for the world's origin, we may gather this essence: the world is a fragment of light released from darkness, an ordered sound drawn from clamour, and a system formed from confusion.

Some who speak of the world's destruction say that just as darkness broke apart and released light, light will one day die and darkness will again cover all. Darkness will avenge itself upon the light that shattered it, appease its ancient hunger, recover its lost dominion, and spread a veil through which none can see. Vast darkness will reign, boasting that it allowed the fragments freedom for a time and has now restored its rule. In that kingdom there will be no citizens, only the sceptre.

Likewise, all that has become language, music, and ordered signs may again oppose and disrupt one another, be cut and joined into alien forms, lose meaning and use, and become noise. One noise will compete with another for dominance until all order collapses into a great clamour. Then there will be neither the skylark's song nor the sweetness of the flute, nor even a discernible difference between humility, entreaty, command, and threat.

Others say that systems will be violated and collide; new systems will attack old ones and the old retaliate. All will become disordered and meaningless until family, government, trade, agriculture, and industry disappear into confusion. That, they say, is the destruction of the world.

Some hold that whatever emerged from something will ultimately be drawn back into it, and that the original source will reign once more.

Those who have devoted thought to this subject belong to many schools. One declares that light born from darkness returns to darkness, ordered sound born from noise returns to noise, order born from confusion yields again to confusion, and life born from emptiness returns to emptiness.

Even within this school many varieties arose and continue to arise. Some concluded that life is impermanent and the world destined for destruction; involvement in it was therefore unnecessary and harmful. They rejected the world and withdrew.

Yet some who renounced the world could not make thought itself die. They tried to discover the benefit of their renunciation and explain it to others. Thus arose the discipline of asceticism.

Time and the conflict of ideas introduced changes of form, interpretation, and feeling even into asceticism. People were compelled to ask: is renunciation truly renunciation, is it pure, or is it merely costume? Answers were given, philosophies clashed, groups formed, and became religions. Religions fought. Darkness, noise, and confusion marched again. Destruction opened its vast mouth and demanded victims until its hunger was fed.

Those who survived eventually understood that the war among themselves had gone far enough and that the struggle against destruction mattered more. They accepted that each person's chosen belief belonged to that person and prevented darkness, noise, and confusion from winning. Religions, customs, systems, and laws remain diverse and reluctant to yield to one another, but we see them refrain from blindly attacking one another.

The bitterness, unrest, and hostility that appear from time to time may be called arrows fired by darkness, noise, and confusion in an attempt to recover their lost ground. The great war has ceased, but the warlike impulse has not died.

There is no condition in which we may rest assured that war will never arise, and whether such peace can ever exist is doubtful. Yet war has been held in check. Fear of its consequences restrains desire.

We cannot say that most people consciously reflect upon this condition before acting. Most are not even troubled by such thoughts. That too may be fortunate, for if the majority remained absorbed in them, the world would resemble a cart whose wheels were locked and could not turn.

Most people's problems are different. Their time, thought, labour, and intelligence are scarcely sufficient for their own immediate difficulties. That is why people cannot personally resolve the questions they consider important, such as political systems, economic plans, and social organisation. Politicians, economists, and social reformers of many kinds have long undertaken that work.

At times, amid countless complications and endless troubles, one feels like saying that all this arose because people stayed in the country instead of renouncing life and retreating to the forest. When weariness and distress strike, we often hear someone say, "Seeing all these troubles, one might simply put on ochre robes, lie down somewhere, and cry Rama, Krishna, Govinda!"

Suppose this ceased to be a passing wave of weariness and became a settled conviction not merely for one or two people but for everyone, or most people, who then entered the ascetic path. What would happen?

The great Bengali poet Tagore said:

> Seven or eight people may abandon home and threshold, live in the forest on roots and tubers, close their eyes, and remain silent. But if everyone follows that path, a great war will arise over asceticism itself. Even bark garments will become scarce; there will be no room in the caves; fruits and roots will not suffice for all. In that congestion, loincloth-clad renunciants will shoot one another with guns.

Troubles cannot therefore be solved through renunciation.

Ascetics can command respect today precisely because renunciation is a field without excessive competition. Neither renunciation nor surrender to sorrow resolves life's troubles. What people need when assaulted by difficulty is endurance, hope that dawn will come, confidence that effort can succeed, and clarity that cooperative labour can produce a condition free of harm.

The wonder is that most people somehow embrace these ideas and keep the world moving. They may be surprised, bewildered, or even frightened when someone states this condition explicitly. Yet their conduct is somehow built upon these convictions and continues to yield benefit.

Among terrifying forces, humanity somehow plants its feet without losing balance. Humans possess power of this kind and measure. It remains hidden, but emerges when needed and often brings great victory.

Humans cannot prevent blinding lightning, yet they tightly close their eyes and largely escape its harm. With eyes open, lightning may destroy sight. Its destructive force does not last long, and merely closing the eyes renders much of it ineffective.

Humans can extinguish a raging fire. Even after fire has caused destruction, they can build anew upon the ruins. Fire is stronger than a person, yet dies when a person sets out to extinguish it and cannot revive merely to mock its conqueror. Humanity instead seems to mock the destructive flame by creating a new structure upon what it ruined. More than that, humans imprison the very fire of destruction inside their hearths and compel it to perform useful work.

Nature's power cannot be measured, though the work of measuring it proceeds vigorously and succeeds in many fields. The age when nature's form and strength terrified people has changed. Humanity now investigates nature, seeks the causes of its power, and tries to convert destructive energy into creative energy.

Humans have split the atom and seek the meaning hidden in the expression "the cosmic universe." They vow to reach the moon and settle on Mars, and act upon that vow. It is no empty boast. Mysteries hidden for ages are being discovered.

A fierce contest has begun between human intelligence and nature's power. Humans speak, so we understand what they think and do. Nature does not speak. How can we know what it thinks, what actions it plans, or what it may undertake?

Yet humanity does not shrink back, thinking, "Why take on this needless work? What if our investigation of one thing produces unforeseen disasters? We live only briefly and our needs are simple; let us remain content with what we have. Why engage in the dangerous game of discovering planets under the pretext of studying nature?"

Instead, people ask why nature should remain so mysterious and how long it should be left unexplored, and they throw themselves energetically into the effort.

The age when astrologers declared that planets possessed power to govern human life has changed. Humans now study those planets and ask which may be put to use. This wondrous method began in the last century and has grown rapidly in this one.

The first claims of those who investigated nature were regarded by many not merely as daring but as insane:

"Can anyone go to the moon?"

"Why not? Not only there; we may even go to Mars."

"Why should we go?"

"Curiosity: an eagerness to know the conditions there, a passion to unravel mystery."

"Call it an itch. Must we undergo such hardship and loss for it? What benefit comes from going there?"

"The principal benefit is bringing the world information it has never known. Knowledge grows."

"Enough! What is already known is more than abundant. Why seek new information? Is it needed? Will plenty of new materials be found?"

"Perhaps sulphur, iron, gold, and even substances we have never discovered before."

"The cost of travelling to the moon and back will be mountainous. Even after spending so much, will enough material be found to make it profitable?"

"Yes. Moreover, once the methods are perfected, the initial cost of lunar travel will gradually decline."

"What if we trust you, reach the moon, and find it merely a tomb?"

"It is not a tomb. I know. Living beings exist there too."

"You speak as though you have already been there."

"I am telling you what research has shown. Come, and you shall see."

In his fictional work *The First Men in the Moon*, the English thinker H. G. Wells presents a conversation along these lines.

Two people board a spacecraft constructed for lunar travel. In Wells's story, the travellers experience almost the same sensations that Gagarin and others later described from spaceflight. The body's weight diminishes; the mind gains confidence. They eat, converse, and even sleep.

They reach the moon and find not even grass anywhere. It appears a wasteland, containing only enormous anthills with wide mouths.

The sun rises, and at once a wonder occurs. Small objects resembling pebbles lying upon the ground begin to move when sunlight strikes them.

"Life! Yes, there is living power on the moon!" they exclaim. As they watch, those moving little forms burst open. Shoots and leaves emerge. They grow second by second into thorny plants. The travellers realise they are not pebbles but seeds.

Here plants take months to grow. On the moon, while one watches, green plants sprout, grow, and densely cover the whole region that had appeared barren.

Wells creates this scene in his story to suggest that life may develop with extraordinary speed on the moon. His idea is that the moon contains more oxygen than the earth, which is why plants that take months to grow here become dense before one's eyes there.

The two descend by leaping into openings like enormous anthills, each as large as a small hill. There they see a five-foot-tall lunar inhabitant herding legless lunar calves weighing eighty feet and measuring two hundred feet in length.

Although the wonders in the tale serve the ideas Wells wished to express, its underlying concept cannot simply be dismissed as fantasy. Today photographs have been taken of the moon. Wells...

---NOTES---
This file is Part 1 of a continuous two-file work concluding in `irulagala_2.md`. Its final word, `வெல்ஸ்`, continues directly into Part 2's opening sentence about what the lunar photograph shows. The reference is to H. G. Wells's *The First Men in the Moon*. The source's unusual dimensions for the imagined lunar calves are retained literally. Scan-confirmed OCR repairs include the opening `பேரிருளிலிருந்து` and the author's name `H.G. வெல்ஸ்`.
